PG.notes("l266", "<p class=\"summary\">Frames the authentication module: why sites need accounts, the roadmap of security levels from plain-text storage up to OAuth, and the 'secrets' app (a Whisper-style anonymous confessions site) built throughout.</p>\n<h3>Why authenticate</h3>\n<ul><li>User-generated data (posts, likes, messages, recipes) must be tied to an individual, so each person gets an account identified by credentials, acting like an ID card in the database.</li><li>Accounts keep private data private: only the owner, after logging in, can see their messages.</li><li>Accounts also gate access by status, e.g. a streaming service unlocks content once a payment flag is set on the user record.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why it is hard</h3>\n<ul><li>Creating sign-up and login is easy; making it secure is the difficult part.</li><li>The module climbs through levels: (1) store email and password in the database, (2) encryption, (3) hashing, (4) salting, (5) cookies and sessions, (6) secrets in environment variables, (7) OAuth / social login such as Google.</li><li>After building everything by hand, third-party helpers such as Passport are introduced to simplify it. Learning the bottom-up version first means you understand what libraries do.</li><li>Skipping ahead to OAuth is possible but not recommended.</li></ul>\n<h3>The app being built</h3>\n<ul><li>A secrets-sharing site: anonymous posts, not linked to an email or username on display.</li><li>Three entry points: a home page with Register and Login buttons, and a secrets page only reachable once authenticated.</li><li>Starter files with styling are supplied so the focus stays on auth logic rather than Bootstrap/CSS.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Treat each 'level' as a deliberately insecure stage that is fixed by the next one; do not ship early levels.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l267", "<p class=\"summary\">Builds the simplest possible authentication: a users table in Postgres, a register route that checks for an existing email then inserts email and password, and a login route that compares the submitted password with the stored one. Works, but stores passwords as plain text, which is the weakness the next levels address.</p>\n<h3>Starting project and prerequisites</h3>\n<ul><li>Download and unzip the starter, cd into it, run npm i. Dependencies: body-parser, ejs, express, pg. Start with nodemon index.js and open localhost:3000.</li><li>Assumes prior modules on Express, EJS and PostgreSQL; the lesson does not re-teach them.</li><li>Pages: home (Login and Register buttons), login, register, and secrets.ejs, a plain page with a heading and a paragraph holding the secret. secrets.ejs is the protected page, to be rendered only after a successful register or login.</li><li>GET routes already just render the pages. The work is in the two POST routes, app.post('/register') and app.post('/login').</li><li>Leave the views alone, but note the form details: both forms use action pointing at their route, method POST, and two inputs named username (holds the email) and password. Those name attributes decide the keys on req.body.</li><li>The starter already has app.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true })); Express's built-in express.urlencoded would work equally.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 1: read the form data</h3>\n<ul><li>Exercise: in both POST routes console.log the submitted values. Use req.body.username and req.body.password.</li><li>Before the fix both are undefined; after it the values appear in the terminal.</li><li>Then rename the local variable to email (const email = req.body.username). Only the right-hand side must match the form's name attribute; the left-hand name is free, and renaming helps tell form fields apart from server variables.</li><li>Delete the console.logs afterwards (they print passwords).</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 2: create the database and table</h3>\n<ul><li>In pgAdmin: right-click Databases, create a database named secrets, open the Query Tool on it and run the CREATE TABLE from queries.sql.</li><li>id is SERIAL PRIMARY KEY (auto-generated). email is VARCHAR NOT NULL UNIQUE so no two users share an address. password is a plain VARCHAR.</li><li>Refresh Schemas &gt; Tables and use View All Rows to see the empty table.</li><li>In index.js: import pg from 'pg', create new pg.Client with user postgres, host localhost, database secrets, your password, port 5432, then call db.connect().</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 3: register route</h3>\n<ul><li>Naive version: db.query('INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES ($1, $2)', [email, password]) then res.render('secrets.ejs'). $1 and $2 are placeholders filled from the array, which keeps values out of the SQL text.</li><li>Registering the same email twice throws a duplicate key error from the UNIQUE constraint. That error is desirable, but it has to be handled.</li><li>Fix: first SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1. If checkResult.rows.length &gt; 0 the user already exists, so respond with a message (send text, render a page, show a message) telling them to log in. Otherwise insert. A more elaborate site could log the user in automatically instead.</li><li>Wrap the queries in try/catch because pg calls can fail (no connection, wrong address or password, database gone); log or handle the error.</li><li>Verify by registering and viewing rows in pgAdmin: a new row with the email and password should appear.</li></ul>\n<h3>Step 4: login route</h3>\n<ul><li>Within try/catch, SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1 with the submitted email.</li><li>result.rows is an array; because emails are unique it has at most one element. result.rows[0] is an object like {id, email, password}. Logging result.rows is a quick way to see this shape.</li><li>If rows.length &gt; 0: take user = result.rows[0], storedPassword = user.password, compare it with the submitted password. Match: res.render('secrets.ejs'). Mismatch: tell them the password is wrong (or redirect to login, or flash a message).</li><li>Else (zero rows): no such user, tell them to register or check the email.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why this level is weak</h3>\n<ul><li>Passwords sit readable in the table. Anyone who reads the database (breach, insider, backup leak) gets every email and password pair, and people reuse them across sites.</li><li>Subsequent levels add encryption, hashing and salting.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">users table</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">CREATE TABLE users (\n  id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY,\n  email VARCHAR(100) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n  password VARCHAR(100)\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Form fields that POST routes read (register.ejs, abridged)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;form action=&quot;/register&quot; method=&quot;POST&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;email&quot; class=&quot;form-control&quot; name=&quot;username&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;input type=&quot;password&quot; class=&quot;form-control&quot; name=&quot;password&quot;&gt;\n  &lt;button type=&quot;submit&quot; class=&quot;btn btn-dark&quot;&gt;Register&lt;/button&gt;\n&lt;/form&gt;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Finished Level 1 server (abridged from the reference)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport pg from &quot;pg&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: &quot;postgres&quot;, host: &quot;localhost&quot;, database: &quot;secrets&quot;,\n  password: &quot;&lt;your password&gt;&quot;, port: 5432,\n});\ndb.connect();\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; res.render(&quot;home.ejs&quot;));\napp.get(&quot;/login&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; res.render(&quot;login.ejs&quot;));\napp.get(&quot;/register&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; res.render(&quot;register.ejs&quot;));\n\napp.post(&quot;/register&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const email = req.body.username;   // form field name is &quot;username&quot;\n  const password = req.body.password;\n  try {\n    const checkResult = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;, [email]);\n    if (checkResult.rows.length &gt; 0) {\n      res.send(&quot;Email already exists. Try logging in.&quot;);\n    } else {\n      await db.query(&quot;INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES ($1, $2)&quot;, [email, password]);\n      res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;);\n    }\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/login&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const email = req.body.username;\n  const password = req.body.password;\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;, [email]);\n    if (result.rows.length &gt; 0) {\n      const user = result.rows[0];\n      if (password === user.password) {\n        res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;);\n      } else {\n        res.send(&quot;Incorrect Password&quot;);\n      }\n    } else {\n      res.send(&quot;User not found. Please register your email.&quot;);\n    }\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`));</pre>\n<h2>The task: Level 1 authentication: register and log in</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Starter project with package.json, index.js (GET routes done, POST routes empty), views including secrets.ejs, queries.sql.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Users can register (saved to Postgres) and log in with email and password to reach secrets.ejs.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>npm i, run nodemon index.js, visit localhost:3000.</li><li>In both POST routes, log req.body.username and req.body.password until they print; then rename to email and password and remove logs.</li><li>Create the secrets database and users table in pgAdmin using queries.sql.</li><li>Import pg, create the client, connect.</li><li>Register: SELECT by email; if found, send an 'already exists' message; else INSERT with $1/$2 and render secrets.ejs; wrap in try/catch.</li><li>Login: SELECT by email; if no row, say user not found; if row, compare passwords; render secrets.ejs on match, else say incorrect password.</li><li>Test: register, check row in pgAdmin, try re-registering, log in with right and wrong password.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> New users appear in the table; duplicate registration is rejected with a message; correct credentials show the secrets page, wrong ones a rejection message.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport pg from &quot;pg&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: &quot;postgres&quot;, host: &quot;localhost&quot;, database: &quot;secrets&quot;,\n  password: &quot;&lt;your password&gt;&quot;, port: 5432,\n});\ndb.connect();\n\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\napp.get(&quot;/&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; res.render(&quot;home.ejs&quot;));\napp.get(&quot;/login&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; res.render(&quot;login.ejs&quot;));\napp.get(&quot;/register&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; res.render(&quot;register.ejs&quot;));\n\napp.post(&quot;/register&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const email = req.body.username;   // form field name is &quot;username&quot;\n  const password = req.body.password;\n  try {\n    const checkResult = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;, [email]);\n    if (checkResult.rows.length &gt; 0) {\n      res.send(&quot;Email already exists. Try logging in.&quot;);\n    } else {\n      await db.query(&quot;INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES ($1, $2)&quot;, [email, password]);\n      res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;);\n    }\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/login&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const email = req.body.username;\n  const password = req.body.password;\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;, [email]);\n    if (result.rows.length &gt; 0) {\n      const user = result.rows[0];\n      if (password === user.password) {\n        res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;);\n      } else {\n        res.send(&quot;Incorrect Password&quot;);\n      }\n    } else {\n      res.send(&quot;User not found. Please register your email.&quot;);\n    }\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});\n\napp.listen(port, () =&gt; console.log(`Server running on port ${port}`));</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Reading req.body.email instead of req.body.username: the field is named username in the form, so it is undefined.</li><li>Duplicate email without a prior check crashes the insert with a unique-constraint error.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference index.js has stray bugs: db.query not awaited in register, render imported from ejs and called instead of res.render, so the response never gets sent; the solution.js file fixes both. Use res.render and await.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Plain text password storage and comparison with === is only acceptable for this teaching level.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Echoing different messages for 'user not found' versus 'wrong password' lets attackers discover which emails are registered.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l268", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains why plain-text password storage is unacceptable, how encryption (Caesar cipher, AES) works with a secret key, why a stolen key defeats it, and how hashing removes the key by using one-way functions that are compared rather than reversed. No code is written yet; implementation comes in later lessons.</p>\n<h3>Why plain text is unacceptable</h3>\n<ul><li>Breaches of well-known companies are regularly reported; leaked data can lead to prosecution and fines, not only unhappy users.</li><li>Ordinary visitors cannot see the backend or database through the browser, but there is plenty of precedent of attackers getting in, so stored passwords need protection.</li></ul>\n<h3>Encryption basics</h3>\n<ul><li>Encryption converts readable data into a coded form (ciphertext) using a cipher and a key, to stop unauthorised reading.</li><li>Caesar cipher: shift each letter along the alphabet by the key. With key 4, A becomes E, so abc becomes efg. Decrypting reverses the shift by the same key. Online tools such as cryptii let you experiment with shift values.</li><li>Every encryption setup has three ingredients: plain text, a key, and a cipher method, producing ciphertext. Whoever has the key can decrypt.</li><li>Weak ciphers are broken easily even without the key (letter-substitution ciphers fall to frequency analysis). A historical case of a substitution cipher being cracked, leading to a treason conviction, is used as the cautionary tale; the book The Code Book by Simon Singh is recommended for more.</li><li>Modern practice uses strong ciphers such as AES-256: same idea, much heavier maths, output longer and hides the original length; decryption needs the secret key (e.g. a passphrase).</li></ul>\n<h3>The weakness of encryption for passwords</h3>\n<ul><li>AES is symmetric: the same key encrypts and decrypts. If an attacker who reaches the database also finds the key (even in an environment variable or on the server), every password can be decrypted at once.</li><li>So the key is the weakest link; hashing removes the need for a key.</li></ul>\n<h3>Hashing</h3>\n<ul><li>A hash function turns a password into a fixed fingerprint (hash) quickly, but is designed so that going backwards is practically impossible with today's computing power.</li><li>Intuition: multiplying 13 by 29 to get 377 is instant, but finding the factors of 377 by trial is slow. Real hash functions add much more complexity to the same one-way property.</li><li>Register: hash the chosen password and store only the hash. Login: hash the typed password with the same function and compare it to the stored hash. Equal hashes imply the same password.</li><li>The server never stores the plain password and cannot recover it; only the user knows it. This also means a 'forgot password' flow must reset, not remind.</li><li>Hashing has its own attack (shown next lesson), which later levels (salting) address.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Caesar cipher in JavaScript, to illustrate key-based encryption</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">function caesar(text, shift) {\n  return [...text.toLowerCase()].map(ch =&gt; {\n    if (ch &lt; &quot;a&quot; || ch &gt; &quot;z&quot;) return ch;            // leave non-letters alone\n    const code = (ch.charCodeAt(0) - 97 + shift + 26) % 26;\n    return String.fromCharCode(code + 97);\n  }).join(&quot;&quot;);\n}\ncaesar(&quot;abc&quot;, 4);   // &quot;efg&quot;  (encrypt, key = 4)\ncaesar(&quot;efg&quot;, -4);  // &quot;abc&quot;  (decrypt with the same key, reversed)</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Hash-based login flow</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">REGISTER:  password --hash()--&gt; hash  --&gt; store hash in DB\nLOGIN:     typed password --hash()--&gt; hash  --&gt; compare with stored hash\n           equal =&gt; same password; the original is never stored or recovered</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Do not rely on a weak or home-made cipher; and a strong cipher is only as safe as the key's secrecy.</li><li>Encrypted is not hashed: encrypted passwords can be reversed by anyone holding the key.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Hashing does not need a key, but plain fast hashes (MD5, SHA-1) are still crackable by guessing; prefer a slow password hash such as bcrypt (used later in the module).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l269", "<p class=\"summary\">Looks at password cracking from the attacker's side so that defences make sense: identical hashes reveal shared passwords, hash tables and dictionary attacks reverse weak passwords quickly, GPUs make guessing fast, and only long, non-dictionary passwords resist. Sets up salting as the next fix.</p>\n<h3>Context and motivation</h3>\n<ul><li>Hashing passwords already puts a site ahead of many real ones. Sites that can email you your actual password are demonstrably storing it in plain text or reversibly encrypted; a plaintext-offenders listing site collects examples. Delete accounts on such sites; developers can use them as a prompt to offer security help.</li><li>Large breaches have exposed millions of accounts, and leaked credentials are traded. Check your own email on a breach-notification service and change any password that appears, everywhere it was reused.</li></ul>\n<h3>How a leaked hash table is attacked</h3>\n<ul><li>A hash function is deterministic: the same password always yields the same hash. That is what makes login verification work, and also what lets an attacker spot users who share a password: identical hashes in the leaked table.</li><li>Attacker builds a hash table: hash lots of likely passwords (123456, qwerty, password, 111111, ...) with the same function, then look up each stolen hash in the table to read off the password.</li><li>Dictionary attack: hash every dictionary word (~150,000), plus phone-number style digits and all character combinations up to about six characters, reaching roughly 20 billion entries.</li><li>GPUs compute hashes in parallel; a modern one does around 20 billion MD5 hashes per second, so a table of that size takes about a second. MD5 is especially fast, so pre-built MD5 tables are common.</li><li>Pre-built tables exist for the most common passwords, drawn from earlier breaches and annual top-password lists. Pasting a hash into a search engine can even reveal the original.</li></ul>\n<h3>What defends a user</h3>\n<ul><li>A strong password's hash will not appear in any table. Cracking cost grows exponentially with length.</li><li>Demo with a password strength checker: a 6-character random password falls in seconds on a desktop to brute force; doubling to 12 characters moves it to decades on a desktop and years on a fast GPU.</li><li>Length matters more than composition rules; short passwords with a capital and a symbol are still weak.</li><li>Avoid dictionary words, place names and anything found in a directory such as phone numbers.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Use a password manager so every site gets a unique long random password.</li></ul>\n<h3>Developer takeaways</h3>\n<ul><li>Fast, unsalted hashes (MD5) are not enough; the next lesson addresses the table and dictionary problem with salting.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Also add rate limiting, breached-password checks and slow password hashes (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2).</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\"><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Toy illustration: identical hashes and a lookup table</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import crypto from &quot;node:crypto&quot;;\n\n// (Extra) a toy dictionary/hash-table attack against unsalted MD5\nconst md5 = s =&gt; crypto.createHash(&quot;md5&quot;).update(s).digest(&quot;hex&quot;);\nconst common = [&quot;123456&quot;, &quot;qwerty&quot;, &quot;password&quot;, &quot;111111&quot;];\nconst table = Object.fromEntries(common.map(p =&gt; [md5(p), p]));   // hash -&gt; password\n\nconst leaked = [\n  { user: &quot;a&quot;, hash: md5(&quot;qwerty&quot;) },\n  { user: &quot;b&quot;, hash: md5(&quot;qwerty&quot;) },     // identical hash =&gt; same password\n  { user: &quot;c&quot;, hash: md5(&quot;x9#Lm!2qZr7@Tb&quot;) },\n];\nfor (const row of leaked) console.log(row.user, table[row.hash] ?? &quot;not in table&quot;);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Identical hashes in a table expose users sharing a password.</li><li>Complexity rules with short length give false confidence.</li><li>Only test cracking techniques on your own data; the lesson is educational.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l270", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains salting and bcrypt salt rounds as the defence against hash-table and brute-force attacks on hashed passwords, then upgrades the Express + Postgres secrets app to hash on register with bcrypt.hash and verify on login with bcrypt.compare.</p>\n<h3>Why plain hashing is not enough</h3>\n<ul><li>Human-chosen passwords are short and often dictionary words, so an attacker can precompute a table mapping common passwords to their hashes and simply look stored hashes up.</li><li>Identical passwords give identical hashes, which is how users sharing a password can be spotted in a leaked table.</li><li>Fast algorithms such as MD5 help the attacker: a GPU can compute billions of MD5 hashes per second, so even a salted MD5 table could be rebuilt quickly.</li></ul>\n<h3>What a salt is</h3>\n<ul><li>A salt is a random string generated per user. It is combined with the password before hashing, so the hash is derived from both.</li><li>Because each user gets a different salt, two users with the same password end up with different hashes, and a precomputed table no longer works.</li><li>The user never sees or remembers the salt. It is stored in the database next to the hash.</li><li>To verify a login: take the typed password, combine it with that user's stored salt, hash it the same way and compare against the stored hash. The plain password is never stored.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> With the bcrypt library the salt is embedded inside the hash string itself (the $2b$10$... prefix holds the algorithm version, cost and salt), so there is no separate salt column to manage.</li></ul>\n<h3>Bcrypt: deliberately slow hashing</h3>\n<ul><li>Bcrypt is an industry-standard password hashing algorithm built on the Blowfish cipher. Its value is that it is slow, so large-scale guessing becomes impractical.</li><li>Rough comparison given: top GPUs manage tens of billions of MD5 hashes per second but only around seventeen thousand bcrypt hashes per second. A salted table that takes seconds with MD5 would take months with bcrypt, so attackers move on to easier targets.</li><li>The npm package is called bcrypt; docs are on npm. It must be added to package.json dependencies and installed with npm install.</li></ul>\n<h3>Salt rounds (cost factor)</h3>\n<ul><li>One round: hash(password + salt). Two rounds: feed the first hash plus the same salt through the hash function again. The number of repetitions is the salt rounds.</li><li>Each +1 on the setting roughly doubles the time to compute a hash. As hardware gets faster, raise the number (e.g. 10 now, 12 later) without changing algorithm or other code.</li><li>Pick a value balancing security against login/register latency. The course uses 10, which was fast enough on the machines tried. Too few rounds (e.g. 2) makes brute force much quicker.</li></ul>\n<h3>Registering: bcrypt.hash</h3>\n<ul><li>Import with import bcrypt from &quot;bcrypt&quot; (project uses ES modules via &quot;type&quot;: &quot;module&quot;) and define const saltRounds = 10 near the top.</li><li>In the POST /register route, after the check that the email does not already exist and just before the INSERT, call bcrypt.hash(password, saltRounds, callback).</li><li>Arguments: the plain text to hash (req.body.password), the salt rounds, and a callback receiving (err, hash).</li><li>The existing INSERT and res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;) move inside the callback, because they must wait for the hash. Since the INSERT is awaited there, the callback is declared async.</li><li>Store hash in the password column instead of the plain password. On err, log it (at minimum console.log/console.error) and do not insert.</li><li>Test by registering a new user: the password column now holds a long unreadable string even though 123456 was typed. Delete earlier rows with plain-text passwords, since they no longer fit the scheme and can never log in.</li></ul>\n<h3>Logging in: bcrypt.compare</h3>\n<ul><li>Hashing the login password again and comparing strings would not work, because salting makes each hash differ. Bcrypt provides compare for this.</li><li>Signature: bcrypt.compare(plainLoginPassword, storedHash, callback). Argument order matters: the plain text first, the stored hash second, so it knows which to hash and which to read the salt from.</li><li>The callback gets (err, result) where result is a boolean. true means match: render secrets.ejs. false: send an Incorrect Password message.</li><li>Rename variables for clarity: loginPassword for what the user typed in the form, storedHashedPassword for the database value.</li><li>The old plain equality check (if/else comparing strings) is deleted and replaced by the compare callback. The 'user not found' branch stays as before.</li><li>Test: correct password logs true and shows the secrets page; a wrong one such as qwerty shows the incorrect password message.</li></ul>\n<h3>Where this leaves the app</h3>\n<ul><li>Even if the database leaks, only salted bcrypt hashes are exposed and there is no feasible way to recover the original passwords.</li><li>Next lesson adds cookies and sessions so a logged-in user does not have to sign in again on every visit.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Imports and salt rounds (top of index.js)</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import express from &quot;express&quot;;\nimport bodyParser from &quot;body-parser&quot;;\nimport pg from &quot;pg&quot;;\nimport bcrypt from &quot;bcrypt&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\nconst saltRounds = 10;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Register route: hash before saving</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.post(&quot;/register&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const email = req.body.username;\n  const password = req.body.password;\n\n  try {\n    const checkResult = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;, [email]);\n\n    if (checkResult.rows.length &gt; 0) {\n      res.send(&quot;Email already exists. Try logging in.&quot;);\n    } else {\n      bcrypt.hash(password, saltRounds, async (err, hash) =&gt; {\n        if (err) {\n          console.error(&quot;Error hashing password:&quot;, err);\n        } else {\n          await db.query(\n            &quot;INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES ($1, $2)&quot;,\n            [email, hash]\n          );\n          res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;);\n        }\n      });\n    }\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Login route: verify with bcrypt.compare</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.post(&quot;/login&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const email = req.body.username;\n  const loginPassword = req.body.password;\n\n  try {\n    const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;, [email]);\n    if (result.rows.length &gt; 0) {\n      const user = result.rows[0];\n      const storedHashedPassword = user.password;\n\n      bcrypt.compare(loginPassword, storedHashedPassword, (err, result) =&gt; {\n        if (err) {\n          console.error(&quot;Error comparing passwords:&quot;, err);\n        } else if (result) {\n          res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;);\n        } else {\n          res.send(&quot;Incorrect Password&quot;);\n        }\n      });\n    } else {\n      res.send(&quot;User not found&quot;);\n    }\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add bcrypt hashing and salting to the secrets app</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Project folder for this lesson (Authentication Lv.2 starter): index.js contains the previous lesson's working email/password app that still stores plain text passwords. package.json already lists bcrypt as a dependency. Postgres database named secrets with a users table (email, password) must be running.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Passwords are hashed with bcrypt (10 salt rounds) on registration and verified with bcrypt.compare on login, so no plain text password is ever stored.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Extract the lesson download, open it in VS Code, cd into the project and run npm install.</li><li>Import bcrypt and add const saltRounds = 10.</li><li>In POST /register, after the existing-user check, wrap the INSERT in bcrypt.hash(password, saltRounds, async (err, hash) =&gt; {...}); insert hash instead of the plain password; log any err.</li><li>Move res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;) into the same callback so it runs after the insert.</li><li>Run node index.js, register a new user and confirm in the database that the password column holds a long hash. Delete any old plain text user rows.</li><li>In POST /login, rename the form value to loginPassword and the database value to storedHashedPassword.</li><li>Replace the string equality if/else with bcrypt.compare(loginPassword, storedHashedPassword, callback); render secrets.ejs when result is true, otherwise send Incorrect Password.</li><li>Test a correct login and a wrong password.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> New users appear in the database with bcrypt hash strings; login works with the right password and shows an incorrect password message for a wrong one.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// see code_examples: register route uses bcrypt.hash(password, saltRounds, cb);\n// login route uses bcrypt.compare(loginPassword, storedHashedPassword, cb)</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Swapping the argument order of bcrypt.compare (plain password first, stored hash second).</li><li>Comparing hash-of-login-password to the stored hash with ===; salting makes this always fail.</li><li>Leaving old plain text rows in the table; they cannot log in under the new scheme.</li><li>Forgetting async on the hash callback when it awaits db.query, or leaving res.render outside the callback so it runs before saving.</li><li>Raising saltRounds too high makes every register and login slow.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference solution logs the generated hash to the console; avoid logging credentials-related values in real apps.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l271", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what cookies and sessions are using a shopping-basket example, then adds persistent login to the secrets app with express-session, Passport and the passport-local strategy: protecting /secrets with req.isAuthenticated(), moving the login check into a verify function, serialising the user into the session and logging users in after registration with req.login.</p>\n<h3>Cookies and sessions: the idea</h3>\n<ul><li>A cookie is a small piece of data the server asks the browser to store. The browser sends it back automatically with each later request to that site.</li><li>Typical flow: browser GETs a page, the user does something (e.g. a POST adding an item to a basket), the server replies and includes a Set-Cookie instruction, and from then on every GET to that server carries the cookie so the server can recognise the visitor and restore their state.</li><li>Shop-style cookies usually hold just an identifier (a session id or token); the real basket data lives on the server and is looked up by that id. Deleting the site's cookies makes the server forget the visitor.</li><li>The same mechanism underlies retargeting ads, since a stored identifier lets sites recall past behaviour.</li><li>A session is the period during which a browser interacts with a server. For authentication it normally starts at login, when a cookie marking the user as authenticated is created; it lasts until logout, when the cookie is destroyed.</li><li>Cookies can be inspected in browser dev tools, Application tab, Cookies section (or in the browser's cookie settings).</li></ul>\n<h3>Packages used</h3>\n<ul><li>express-session: middleware that creates a session and stores the session id in a cookie, so returning users bypass the login form.</li><li>passport: authentication framework for Node with hundreds of interchangeable strategies (Google, Facebook, GitHub, etc.), which can be plugged in one after another.</li><li>passport-local: the strategy for username/email plus password, which is what the app uses now.</li><li>All three are in the lesson's package.json, so npm install adds them. The app also still needs bcrypt, pg, body-parser, ejs and express.</li></ul>\n<h3>Configuring express-session</h3>\n<ul><li>import session from &quot;express-session&quot; and register it with app.use(session({...})) before anything that depends on it.</li><li>secret: string used to sign the session cookie. Required. Any string works, but treat it as a key (the next lesson moves it into an environment variable).</li><li>resave: false: do not force the session to be written back to the store on every request if unchanged.</li><li>saveUninitialized: true: store sessions even if nothing has been put in them yet.</li><li>Default store is server memory, so sessions vanish when the server restarts. For persistence use a store backed by Postgres (look up a session store package).</li><li>cookie: { maxAge: ms } sets the expiry in milliseconds. 1000 * 60 * 60 * 24 gives one day. Without it the cookie expires when the browser is closed, so reopening the browser asks for login again.</li></ul>\n<h3>Initialising Passport</h3>\n<ul><li>import passport from &quot;passport&quot;. After the session middleware, add app.use(passport.initialize()) and app.use(passport.session()).</li><li>Order matters because middleware runs top to bottom: the session must exist before Passport's session support runs, otherwise authentication will not work.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Both lines must also sit above the routes that use req.isAuthenticated or req.user.</li></ul>\n<h3>Protecting the /secrets route</h3>\n<ul><li>Instead of rendering secrets.ejs straight from the login/register handlers, add app.get(&quot;/secrets&quot;, ...).</li><li>Inside, use req.isAuthenticated() (added to the request by Passport). If true, res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;); otherwise res.redirect(&quot;/login&quot;).</li><li>Visiting /secrets with no session redirects to /login. After login the session cookie lets the user return to /secrets directly, even in a new tab.</li></ul>\n<h3>The local strategy and verify function</h3>\n<ul><li>import { Strategy } from &quot;passport-local&quot;. Register it near the bottom with passport.use(new Strategy(async function verify(username, password, cb) {...})).</li><li>Passport calls verify whenever a request is authenticated. By default it reads fields named username and password from the submitted form, so the form inputs must have those exact name attributes. This replaces manually reading req.body.</li><li>The function is async because it awaits the database query.</li><li>Move the old login logic here: query users WHERE email = $1 with [username]; if a row is found, take its stored hash and run bcrypt.compare(password, storedHashedPassword, callback).</li><li>Report results through the Passport callback cb(err, user): success is cb(null, user) with the user row; wrong password is cb(null, false), which is not an error, just a failed authentication; an error is cb(err); an unknown email is also returned through cb (the reference passes a 'User not found' value).</li><li>The user object passed to cb becomes req.user later, containing id, email and password hash from the row.</li><li>The docs for passport-local (username and password tutorial, verify section) are the source for this pattern. The docs use Node's crypto for hashing, while this course uses bcrypt.</li></ul>\n<h3>serializeUser and deserializeUser</h3>\n<ul><li>passport.serializeUser((user, cb) =&gt; cb(null, user)) decides what gets stored in the session when someone logs in.</li><li>passport.deserializeUser((user, cb) =&gt; cb(null, user)) turns the stored data back into the object exposed as req.user on later requests.</li><li>Here the whole user object is saved, which is simple for teaching. (Extra) In larger apps the usual practice is to serialise only the id and load the full user from the database in deserialize.</li></ul>\n<h3>Login route via passport.authenticate</h3>\n<ul><li>The POST /login handler no longer needs its own function body: app.post(&quot;/login&quot;, passport.authenticate(&quot;local&quot;, { successRedirect: &quot;/secrets&quot;, failureRedirect: &quot;/login&quot; })).</li><li>passport.authenticate is middleware that triggers the registered local strategy. On success it starts the session and redirects to /secrets; on failure it redirects back to /login.</li><li>Console-logging req.user in the /secrets route shows the stored user object after login. The Application tab shows a new cookie for localhost:3000.</li></ul>\n<h3>Registration with req.login</h3>\n<ul><li>Change the INSERT to end with RETURNING * so the new row comes back in result.rows.</li><li>Take const user = result.rows[0] and call req.login(user, callback) (lowercase i, provided by Passport). It places the user in the session exactly like a normal login, with serialization.</li><li>In the callback, handle any error (log it), then res.redirect(&quot;/secrets&quot;) so the new user lands on the protected page already authenticated.</li><li>Because req.user is set everywhere after authentication, any route can check who is logged in and use their data.</li></ul>\n<h3>Why this matters</h3>\n<ul><li>Persisting sessions adds convenience rather than extra cryptographic security, but it is how modern sites keep users logged in.</li><li>The session secret in the code is sensitive; next lesson shows how to keep it out of the repository via environment variables.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Session and Passport middleware, in the required order</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import session from &quot;express-session&quot;;\nimport passport from &quot;passport&quot;;\nimport { Strategy } from &quot;passport-local&quot;;\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    secret: &quot;TOPSECRETWORD&quot;,\n    resave: false,\n    saveUninitialized: true,\n    cookie: {\n      maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 24, // one day in milliseconds\n    },\n  })\n);\napp.use(bodyParser.urlencoded({ extended: true }));\napp.use(express.static(&quot;public&quot;));\n\napp.use(passport.initialize());\napp.use(passport.session());</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Protected route and login via Passport</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">app.get(&quot;/secrets&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (req.isAuthenticated()) {\n    res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;);\n  } else {\n    res.redirect(&quot;/login&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\napp.post(\n  &quot;/login&quot;,\n  passport.authenticate(&quot;local&quot;, {\n    successRedirect: &quot;/secrets&quot;,\n    failureRedirect: &quot;/login&quot;,\n  })\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Register: save user, then log them in</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">bcrypt.hash(password, saltRounds, async (err, hash) =&gt; {\n  if (err) {\n    console.error(&quot;Error hashing password:&quot;, err);\n  } else {\n    const result = await db.query(\n      &quot;INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES ($1, $2) RETURNING *&quot;,\n      [email, hash]\n    );\n    const user = result.rows[0];\n    req.login(user, (err) =&gt; {\n      console.log(&quot;success&quot;);\n      res.redirect(&quot;/secrets&quot;);\n    });\n  }\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Local strategy, plus serialise/deserialise</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">passport.use(\n  new Strategy(async function verify(username, password, cb) {\n    try {\n      const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;, [username]);\n      if (result.rows.length &gt; 0) {\n        const user = result.rows[0];\n        const storedHashedPassword = user.password;\n        bcrypt.compare(password, storedHashedPassword, (err, valid) =&gt; {\n          if (err) {\n            return cb(err);\n          } else if (valid) {\n            return cb(null, user);   // authenticated\n          } else {\n            return cb(null, false);  // wrong password\n          }\n        });\n      } else {\n        return cb(&quot;User not found&quot;);\n      }\n    } catch (err) {\n      return cb(err);\n    }\n  })\n);\n\npassport.serializeUser((user, cb) =&gt; cb(null, user));\npassport.deserializeUser((user, cb) =&gt; cb(null, user));</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add cookies and sessions with Passport to the secrets app</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Project 'Sessions &amp; Cookies' (previous lesson's code): bcrypt-based register and login routes that render secrets.ejs directly. package.json already lists express-session, passport and passport-local. Postgres secrets database with users table.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Users stay logged in across tabs and visits via a session cookie, /secrets is only reachable when authenticated, and registering logs the new user in.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Extract, open in VS Code, cd into the folder and run npm install.</li><li>Import session, passport and Strategy (from passport-local).</li><li>Add app.use(session({ secret, resave: false, saveUninitialized: true })) above everything else, then app.use(passport.initialize()) and app.use(passport.session()) after it.</li><li>Add GET /secrets that renders secrets.ejs only if req.isAuthenticated(), else redirects to /login.</li><li>Create passport.use(new Strategy(async function verify(username, password, cb) {...})); move the login query and bcrypt.compare logic in and report results via cb.</li><li>Confirm login.ejs inputs are named username and password so Passport finds them.</li><li>Add serializeUser and deserializeUser.</li><li>Replace the POST /login body with passport.authenticate(&quot;local&quot;, { successRedirect: &quot;/secrets&quot;, failureRedirect: &quot;/login&quot; }).</li><li>Update POST /register: INSERT ... RETURNING *, take rows[0], call req.login(user, cb), redirect to /secrets.</li><li>Test: visit /secrets while logged out (redirects), log in (lands on secrets, req.user logged), refresh, open a new tab, inspect the cookie in dev tools.</li><li>Optionally add cookie: { maxAge: 1000 * 60 * 60 * 24 } to the session options and confirm login survives closing the browser.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Logging in or registering redirects to /secrets; direct visits to /secrets work while the session lasts and redirect to /login otherwise.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// Key pieces are shown in code_examples. Reference structure:\napp.use(session({ secret: &quot;TOPSECRETWORD&quot;, resave: false, saveUninitialized: true }));\napp.use(passport.initialize());\napp.use(passport.session());\n\napp.get(&quot;/secrets&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (req.isAuthenticated()) res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;);\n  else res.redirect(&quot;/login&quot;);\n});\n\napp.post(&quot;/login&quot;, passport.authenticate(&quot;local&quot;, { successRedirect: &quot;/secrets&quot;, failureRedirect: &quot;/login&quot; }));\n\n// register: INSERT ... RETURNING * -&gt; req.login(user, cb) -&gt; res.redirect(&quot;/secrets&quot;)\n// passport.use(new Strategy(async function verify(username, password, cb) { ... }));\n// passport.serializeUser / deserializeUser pass the user through unchanged</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Registering passport.initialize()/passport.session() before the session middleware.</li><li>Form field names not being username and password, so verify receives nothing.</li><li>Mixing up req.login (lowercase i, needs a user object) with unrelated names such as login.</li><li>Wrong password should call cb(null, false), not cb(err).</li><li>Memory session store loses sessions on server restart.</li><li>Forgetting maxAge, so the login ends when the browser closes.</li><li>Reference repo quirks: the already-registered branch calls req.redirect(&quot;/login&quot;) which should be res.redirect; the logout route uses next without declaring it as a parameter; the DB password string has a trailing space; the reference does not set cookie maxAge (the lecture demonstrates it).</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l272", "<p class=\"summary\">Shows why API keys and session secrets must not live in source code, and how to move them into a .env file loaded by the dotenv package and read through process.env. Also covers .gitignore so the .env file never reaches GitHub, and ends with an exercise moving the Postgres connection settings into environment variables.</p>\n<h3>Why secrets must stay out of the code</h3>\n<ul><li>This protects the developer's own credentials rather than the users' data. A session secret, API key or cloud key written in index.js ends up on GitHub or on a server, where it is easy to leak.</li><li>Bots scan public repositories for exposed keys. Stolen cloud keys can be used to run compute-heavy jobs (such as crypto mining), producing large bills; the lecture cites a developer who accidentally pushed AWS keys and got a bill above $3,000 in a short time (refunded in that case, but not guaranteed).</li><li>The app still needs the secrets to run, and code still needs to be shared through version control, so the secrets must be stored separately from the code.</li></ul>\n<h3>What environment variables give you</h3>\n<ul><li>Convenience: values that change between deployments can be altered without editing code files.</li><li>Security: keys and passwords live in a separate file or in the host environment, not alongside the code that gets published.</li><li>In development they are kept in a simple text file called .env, loaded by the dotenv package (one of the most downloaded npm packages).</li></ul>\n<h3>Creating the .env file</h3>\n<ul><li>Add dotenv to the project (it is already in the lesson's package.json) and run npm install.</li><li>Create a file named exactly .env at the project root (no name before the dot). A gear-style icon appears in VS Code if an icon extension such as vscode-icons is installed.</li><li>Format: NAME=&quot;value&quot;. The name is conventionally ALL_CAPS with underscores between words, and the line has no const/var keyword because this is not JavaScript.</li><li>No spaces around the equals sign, no commas or semicolons. Each variable goes on its own line.</li><li>Cut the secret out of index.js and paste it in as e.g. SESSION_SECRET=&quot;...&quot;.</li></ul>\n<h3>Loading and using the variables</h3>\n<ul><li>import env from &quot;dotenv&quot; and call env.config() near the top of index.js. This reads .env and adds its entries to process.env.</li><li>Replace the hard-coded value with process.env.SESSION_SECRET (process is the Node process object, env is the property holding the environment, and the name after it must match the .env name).</li><li>Call config() before the code that reads the variables, otherwise they are undefined.</li><li>The app behaves exactly as before (login and sessions still work) but the secret no longer appears in index.js.</li></ul>\n<h3>Keeping .env out of Git with .gitignore</h3>\n<ul><li>A file named .gitignore (one word, no spaces) lists files and folders Git should not track or upload.</li><li>GitHub publishes a standard Node .gitignore template. It includes node_modules (huge, restored by npm install) and .env files (including variants for local, production and so on).</li><li>Demo on two throwaway projects: first, a repo created with a .env and no .gitignore and published publicly exposed the .env on GitHub; second, copying the template into .gitignore before the first commit left .env out of the repo.</li><li>Rule: add .gitignore at the very start of a project, before the first commit. (Extra) If a secret was already committed, remove it from history and rotate the key, because deleting the file later does not erase earlier commits.</li><li>VS Code's Source Control tab can initialise a repository, stage, commit and publish the branch.</li></ul>\n<h3>Exercise: database settings</h3>\n<ul><li>Another thing that belongs in the environment is the database connection: user, host, database name, password and port. A real database is usually hosted remotely, so these values grant access to it.</li><li>Add PG_USER, PG_HOST, PG_DATABASE, PG_PASSWORD and PG_PORT to .env as strings with no spaces, then replace each property of the pg.Client config with the matching process.env.PG_* value.</li><li>After this, index.js reveals neither the DB credentials nor the session secret.</li></ul>\n<h3>Next</h3>\n<ul><li>The following lessons hand authentication to Google using OAuth, so the app no longer stores user passwords itself.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">.env file (values are placeholders)</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">SESSION_SECRET=&quot;TOPSECRETWORD&quot;\nPG_USER=&quot;postgres&quot;\nPG_HOST=&quot;localhost&quot;\nPG_DATABASE=&quot;secrets&quot;\nPG_PASSWORD=&quot;your-db-password&quot;\nPG_PORT=&quot;5432&quot;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Loading dotenv and using process.env in index.js</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import env from &quot;dotenv&quot;;\n\nconst app = express();\nconst port = 3000;\nconst saltRounds = 10;\nenv.config();\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET,\n    resave: false,\n    saveUninitialized: true,\n  })\n);\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: process.env.PG_USER,\n  host: process.env.PG_HOST,\n  database: process.env.PG_DATABASE,\n  password: process.env.PG_PASSWORD,\n  port: process.env.PG_PORT,\n});\ndb.connect();</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Minimal .gitignore (extract of the standard Node template)</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">node_modules/\n.env\n.env.local\n.env.production</pre>\n<h2>The task: Move the session secret and database settings into .env</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Project 'Environment Variables': the cookies-and-sessions app with dotenv already in package.json; secret and Postgres credentials hard-coded in index.js.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> No secret or credential appears in index.js; all are read from process.env via a git-ignored .env file.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>cd into the project and run npm install.</li><li>Create a .env file in the project root.</li><li>Move the session secret into SESSION_SECRET=&quot;...&quot;.</li><li>Import dotenv (import env from &quot;dotenv&quot;) and call env.config() before using the variables.</li><li>Replace the secret in the session options with process.env.SESSION_SECRET and confirm login and sessions still work.</li><li>Add PG_USER, PG_HOST, PG_DATABASE, PG_PASSWORD and PG_PORT to .env.</li><li>Replace each pg.Client option with its process.env.PG_* counterpart.</li><li>Create a .gitignore (copy the GitHub Node template) so .env and node_modules are not committed.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> The app runs and behaves identically; index.js contains only process.env references and .env is excluded from Git.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">env.config();\n\napp.use(\n  session({\n    secret: process.env.SESSION_SECRET,\n    resave: false,\n    saveUninitialized: true,\n  })\n);\n\nconst db = new pg.Client({\n  user: process.env.PG_USER,\n  host: process.env.PG_HOST,\n  database: process.env.PG_DATABASE,\n  password: process.env.PG_PASSWORD,\n  port: process.env.PG_PORT,\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Spaces around = in .env, or stray commas/semicolons.</li><li>Calling process.env.X before env.config() has run.</li><li>Committing .env before creating .gitignore.</li><li>Misspelling a variable name, so process.env returns undefined.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference repo's .env file is not included (correctly ignored), so you must write your own with the keys above.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Variable values are always strings, so PG_PORT arrives as the string &quot;5432&quot;; pg accepts this.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l273", "<p class=\"summary\">Written setup guide for creating your own Google OAuth client ID and secret in Google Cloud Console, needed for the 'Sign in with Google' lesson that follows. The instructor's own credentials cannot be reused.</p>\n<h3>Setup checklist in Google Cloud Console</h3>\n<ul><li>Sign in to console.cloud.google.com with a Google account and create a new project (named Secrets here); no organisation is needed.</li><li>Open APIs &amp; Services, then Credentials, from the navigation menu.</li><li>Configure the OAuth consent screen first: this is the page users see when granting your app access. Choose the External user type; staying in testing mode avoids Google's app verification. Fill in app name, support email and developer contact email. No logo or domain is required.</li><li>Scopes define which user data your app receives. Only the email is needed, so add just the email scope via Add or Remove Scopes. Test users are not required for this setup.</li><li>Back in Credentials, choose Create Credentials, then OAuth client ID, and set the application type to Web application.</li><li>Authorised JavaScript origin: http://localhost:3000.</li><li>Authorised redirect URI: http://localhost:3000/auth/google/secrets. This is where Google sends the user after they authenticate, and it must match the callback route used in the Express app (which then shows the secrets page).</li><li>Click Create, then copy the Client ID and Client Secret for use in the next lesson.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Store those two values in the project's .env file (for example GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID and GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET) rather than in index.js, using the approach from the previous lesson, and keep .env git-ignored.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Google's console layout changes often, so menu names may differ slightly; the OAuth consent screen may now appear under an Auth Platform or Google Auth section.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Values to enter in the Google client form and keep in .env (placeholders)</p><pre data-lang=\"txt\">Authorised JavaScript origin:  http://localhost:3000\nAuthorised redirect URI:      http://localhost:3000/auth/google/secrets\n\n# .env\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=&quot;&lt;your client id&gt;&quot;\nGOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=&quot;&lt;your client secret&gt;&quot;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Create Google OAuth credentials</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> A Google account and the secrets app from the previous lessons.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Obtain a Client ID and Client Secret configured for http://localhost:3000.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Open Google Cloud Console and create a project called Secrets.</li><li>Go to APIs &amp; Services, Credentials.</li><li>Configure the consent screen: External, app name and emails, email scope only.</li><li>Create an OAuth client ID of type Web application.</li><li>Add http://localhost:3000 as a JavaScript origin and http://localhost:3000/auth/google/secrets as a redirect URI.</li><li>Create it and copy the Client ID and Client Secret.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> You hold a Client ID and Client Secret ready for the Sign in with Google lesson.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Redirect URI not matching exactly (port, path or http vs https), which gives a redirect_uri_mismatch error.</li><li>Committing the client secret to Git.</li><li>Trying to reuse the instructor's credentials from the video.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l274", "<p class=\"summary\">Explains what OAuth is and why delegating login to a big provider is safer than storing passwords yourself, then adds a Google login strategy to the Passport/Express/Postgres Secrets app, along with the callback route and a working logout route.</p>\n<h3>What OAuth is</h3>\n<ul><li>OAuth is an open standard for token-based authorization: a user lets one site (the client) access chosen data held by another service without handing over their password to the client.</li><li>Two uses: pull data from the provider (e.g. a friends list or Gmail contacts to pre-populate a new social app), and authenticate the user by letting a trusted company verify identity.</li><li>Main security benefit for the developer: password storage, hashing, salting, peppering and database protection become the provider's problem, which reduces your liability and development time. Many sites now offer only social login.</li><li>You can run it next to the email/password local strategy, or instead of it.</li></ul>\n<h3>Three properties that make OAuth special</h3>\n<ul><li>Granular access: the app requests only specific scopes (e.g. profile and email only, versus also a friends list).</li><li>Read-only or read-and-write access: the app asks for read access to data, or additionally write access (e.g. posting on the user's behalf).</li><li>Revocable: the user can withdraw the permission from the provider's own account settings without visiting your site.</li></ul>\n<h3>The OAuth flow step by step</h3>\n<ul><li>1. Register the app with the provider (Google Cloud console, done in the previous setup lesson). You receive a client ID and client secret; your site is the 'client'.</li><li>2. The user clicks 'sign in with Google' and is sent to the provider's own page, so credentials are only ever typed on a domain they trust.</li><li>3. The user logs in and reviews the permissions (scopes) the app asks for, then consents.</li><li>4. The provider redirects back to your registered callback URL with an authorization code, which proves the user authenticated.</li><li>5. Optionally, the code is exchanged for an access token, which is stored and used later to request the user's data. (Extra) In the Passport strategy this exchange is done by the library; it hands you accessToken, refreshToken and profile.</li><li>Auth code vs access token: the code is single-use proof of login (like a one-time ticket); the access token lasts much longer and is the credential for fetching data (like a season pass).</li></ul>\n<h3>Configuring the Google strategy</h3>\n<ul><li>The only new package is passport-google-oauth2; install with npm install (already in package.json). Import it as GoogleStrategy.</li><li>.env must hold GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID and GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET from the earlier setup lesson; spelling of the env names and option names must match exactly.</li><li>Register it with passport.use('google', new GoogleStrategy(options, verifyCallback)), placed below the local strategy. The first argument is the strategy name.</li><li>Options object: clientID and clientSecret (from process.env); callbackURL (must exactly equal one of the redirect URIs registered in the Google console, here http://localhost:3000/auth/google/secrets); userProfileURL (Google's userinfo endpoint, https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/userinfo, used to fetch the profile).</li><li>Verify callback signature: (accessToken, refreshToken, profile, cb). The course does not use accessToken or refreshToken; the refresh token would help keep a user's access alive. profile contains name, picture, email and more; first it is just console.logged to inspect it.</li><li>cb follows Passport convention: cb(err) on error, cb(null, user) on success, cb(null, false) on a failed login.</li></ul>\n<h3>Naming strategies and the two routes</h3>\n<ul><li>With more than one strategy, name each one ('local', 'google') in passport.use and pass the matching name to passport.authenticate so Passport runs the right verify function. With only one strategy the name is optional.</li><li>GET /auth/google: both the register and login pages' 'Sign in/up with Google' buttons point here. It runs passport.authenticate('google', { scope: ['profile','email'] }), which redirects to Google's consent screen and tells the user which data is being requested.</li><li>GET /auth/google/secrets: this is the callback URL Google redirects to after consent. It runs passport.authenticate('google', { successRedirect: '/secrets', failureRedirect: '/login' }); this is the moment the verify callback executes.</li><li>For an unverified app Google shows a warning screen. Verification takes weeks, which is irrelevant on localhost for a tutorial but required for production; click Continue past the warning.</li></ul>\n<h3>Saving the Google user in the database</h3>\n<ul><li>Inside the verify callback, use try/catch with async/await.</li><li>Look up by email: SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1 with [profile.email].</li><li>If result.rows.length === 0 the user is new: INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES ($1, $2) with [profile.email, 'google'], then call cb(null, newUser.rows[0]).</li><li>Else the user exists: cb(null, result.rows[0]) so they are not asked to log in again.</li><li>In catch: cb(err).</li><li>There is no password from Google, so the password column holds a placeholder. Choices: a marker string like 'google' (also blocks logging in via the local form with that email in a meaningful way, as bcrypt compare will fail), or the Google user id. It is the developer's choice.</li><li>The user passed to cb goes through the existing serializeUser/deserializeUser so req.user and req.isAuthenticated() work exactly as for local logins; the session persists across browser restarts.</li></ul>\n<h3>Comparing against the solution in VS Code</h3>\n<ul><li>Right-click your index.js and choose Select for Compare, then right-click solution.js and choose Compare with Selected. Red lines are in yours but not the solution; green lines exist only in the solution. Ordering differences also show up. The env-driven Google strategy block should match.</li></ul>\n<h3>Logout route</h3>\n<ul><li>The secrets page's Log Out link requests GET /logout.</li><li>Use req.logout (lowercase) with a callback: if err, handle it; otherwise res.redirect('/'). It ends the session login, so /secrets then redirects to /login.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> Since Passport 0.6 logout is asynchronous and requires the callback; forward errors with next(err) so Express handles them.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">Google strategy added next to the local strategy</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import GoogleStrategy from &quot;passport-google-oauth2&quot;;\n\npassport.use(\n  &quot;google&quot;,\n  new GoogleStrategy(\n    {\n      clientID: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID,\n      clientSecret: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET,\n      callbackURL: &quot;http://localhost:3000/auth/google/secrets&quot;,\n      userProfileURL: &quot;https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/userinfo&quot;,\n    },\n    async (accessToken, refreshToken, profile, cb) =&gt; {\n      try {\n        const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;, [\n          profile.email,\n        ]);\n        if (result.rows.length === 0) {\n          const newUser = await db.query(\n            &quot;INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES ($1, $2) RETURNING *&quot;,\n            [profile.email, &quot;google&quot;]\n          );\n          return cb(null, newUser.rows[0]);\n        } else {\n          return cb(null, result.rows[0]);\n        }\n      } catch (err) {\n        return cb(err);\n      }\n    }\n  )\n);</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Routes: Google redirect, callback, logout</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// 1) Button on login/register pages links here; sends the user to Google\napp.get(\n  &quot;/auth/google&quot;,\n  passport.authenticate(&quot;google&quot;, {\n    scope: [&quot;profile&quot;, &quot;email&quot;],\n  })\n);\n\n// 2) Google redirects back here after consent\napp.get(\n  &quot;/auth/google/secrets&quot;,\n  passport.authenticate(&quot;google&quot;, {\n    successRedirect: &quot;/secrets&quot;,\n    failureRedirect: &quot;/login&quot;,\n  })\n);\n\n// 3) Logout (lowercase req.logout, needs a callback in Passport 0.6+)\napp.get(&quot;/logout&quot;, (req, res, next) =&gt; {\n  req.logout((err) =&gt; {\n    if (err) return next(err);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/&quot;);\n  });\n});\n\n// Name the local strategy too, now that two exist\npassport.use(&quot;local&quot;, new Strategy(async function verify(username, password, cb) { /* unchanged */ }));</pre>\n<h2>The task: Add Sign in with Google to the Secrets app</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Express + Passport + pg project from the previous lessons with local strategy, sessions, bcrypt, .env filled with Google credentials, and login/register pages that already contain Google buttons linking to /auth/google. index.js lacks the Google strategy and the logout route; solution.js is the finished file.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Let users register or log in via Google, store them in the users table, keep the session, and log out.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Run npm install and confirm GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID and GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET are in .env.</li><li>Import GoogleStrategy from passport-google-oauth2.</li><li>Name the existing strategy 'local' in passport.use.</li><li>Add passport.use('google', new GoogleStrategy({...options}, verify)) below it; first just console.log(profile).</li><li>Add GET /auth/google with scope ['profile','email'].</li><li>Add GET /auth/google/secrets with successRedirect '/secrets' and failureRedirect '/login'.</li><li>Test by clicking the Google button, passing the unverified-app warning, and inspecting the logged profile.</li><li>Replace the log with the select-then-insert logic and call cb with the user row.</li><li>Add GET /logout using req.logout with a callback and redirect to '/'.</li><li>Compare with solution.js and test: new Google user appears in the table, session survives a browser restart, logout blocks /secrets.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Google users appear in the users table with password 'google', land on /secrets after consent, and Log Out returns to the home page.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">import GoogleStrategy from &quot;passport-google-oauth2&quot;;\n\npassport.use(\n  &quot;google&quot;,\n  new GoogleStrategy(\n    {\n      clientID: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID,\n      clientSecret: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET,\n      callbackURL: &quot;http://localhost:3000/auth/google/secrets&quot;,\n      userProfileURL: &quot;https://www.googleapis.com/oauth2/v3/userinfo&quot;,\n    },\n    async (accessToken, refreshToken, profile, cb) =&gt; {\n      try {\n        const result = await db.query(&quot;SELECT * FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;, [\n          profile.email,\n        ]);\n        if (result.rows.length === 0) {\n          const newUser = await db.query(\n            &quot;INSERT INTO users (email, password) VALUES ($1, $2) RETURNING *&quot;,\n            [profile.email, &quot;google&quot;]\n          );\n          return cb(null, newUser.rows[0]);\n        } else {\n          return cb(null, result.rows[0]);\n        }\n      } catch (err) {\n        return cb(err);\n      }\n    }\n  )\n);</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>callbackURL must match the redirect URI registered in Google console exactly (port, path, scheme), otherwise Google returns an error.</li><li>Typos in option names or env variable names fail silently or give a confusing OAuth error.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> The reference solution.js omits RETURNING * on the Google INSERT, so newUser.rows[0] is undefined and cb is called with no user. Add RETURNING * (as shown above) so req.user gets the new row. Its logout handler also calls next without declaring it in the route parameters.</li><li>Use lowercase req.logout, not Logout-style variants, and always provide the callback.</li><li>Not naming strategies when two exist makes it unclear which verify function runs.</li><li>Unverified-app warning is expected in development, not an error.</li><li>A stored placeholder password like 'google' is not a hash; make sure the local login can never accept it for real users.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l275", "<p class=\"summary\">Final challenge of the Secrets app: add a secret column, make secrets.ejs render a per-user secret, protect a submit page, and save the submitted text against the logged-in user's row. The lecture sets the challenge, then walks through the solution.</p>\n<h3>Feature overview</h3>\n<ul><li>Until now the secret on secrets.ejs was fixed text in a paragraph. The goal is to let each authenticated user store and view their own secret.</li><li>Storage is kept simple: one extra column on the existing users table, so a secret lives on the same row as its owner.</li><li>Flow: login (local or Google) -&gt; /secrets shows the user's secret (or a default) -&gt; 'Submit a Secret' button -&gt; GET /submit shows a form -&gt; POST /submit saves the text -&gt; redirect back to /secrets.</li></ul>\n<h3>Database change</h3>\n<ul><li>Add a text column: ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN secret TEXT; (run in pgAdmin or psql).</li><li>Rows for users who have not submitted anything have NULL in this column, which is what the default-message logic relies on.</li></ul>\n<h3>Template change</h3>\n<ul><li>In secrets.ejs replace the hard-coded paragraph text with an output tag, &lt;%= secret %&gt;, so the server passes the value in when rendering.</li><li>submit.ejs contains a form with action /submit and method POST and a text input whose name attribute is secret, so the server reads it as req.body.secret (body-parser's urlencoded middleware is already enabled).</li></ul>\n<h3>Protecting GET /submit</h3>\n<ul><li>Passport adds req.isAuthenticated() to every request. Use it: if true, render submit.ejs; otherwise redirect to /login.</li><li>This is the same guard pattern already used for /secrets.</li></ul>\n<h3>POST /submit</h3>\n<ul><li>Read the text from req.body.secret.</li><li>Identify the owner via req.user. Both the local and Google strategies pass the full user row to cb, which is serialized into the session and deserialized into req.user, so req.user.email is available in every route.</li><li>Run UPDATE users SET secret = $1 WHERE email = $2 with [secret, req.user.email]. The WHERE clause restricts the update to one user; it replaces any previous secret.</li><li>The handler must be async because of await db.query. Wrap in try/catch (the course just console.logs errors), then redirect to /secrets (a GET) so the new value is displayed.</li></ul>\n<h3>Updated GET /secrets</h3>\n<ul><li>Make the handler async. Inside the authenticated branch query SELECT secret FROM users WHERE email = $1 with [req.user.email]; the parameter must be wrapped in an array.</li><li>result.rows[0].secret holds the value. If truthy, res.render('secrets.ejs', { secret }); otherwise render with a default string (the lecture uses a joke default; a prompt like 'You should submit a secret!' also works).</li><li>The second argument of res.render is the data object whose keys become variables in the EJS template.</li><li>Unauthenticated visitors are redirected to /login.</li></ul>\n<h3>Testing and next steps</h3>\n<ul><li>Log in, submit a secret, confirm the row updated in the database, log out and back in to prove it persists in the database, not in the session.</li><li>Remember to fill GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID and GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET in .env for Google login.</li><li>Suggested practice: add authentication to an earlier project of your own.</li></ul>\n<h2>Code examples</h2>\n<p class=\"caption\">SQL to add the column</p><pre data-lang=\"sql\">ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN secret TEXT;</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">Three routes that complete the app</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// GET /secrets: show the logged-in user's own secret\napp.get(&quot;/secrets&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (req.isAuthenticated()) {\n    try {\n      const result = await db.query(\n        &quot;SELECT secret FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;,\n        [req.user.email]\n      );\n      const secret = result.rows[0].secret;\n      if (secret) {\n        res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;, { secret: secret });\n      } else {\n        res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;, { secret: &quot;Jack Bauer is my hero.&quot; });\n      }\n    } catch (err) {\n      console.log(err);\n    }\n  } else {\n    res.redirect(&quot;/login&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\n// GET /submit: only for authenticated users\napp.get(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (req.isAuthenticated()) {\n    res.render(&quot;submit.ejs&quot;);\n  } else {\n    res.redirect(&quot;/login&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\n// POST /submit: store the secret on the user's own row\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const submittedSecret = req.body.secret;\n  try {\n    await db.query(&quot;UPDATE users SET secret = $1 WHERE email = $2&quot;, [\n      submittedSecret,\n      req.user.email,\n    ]);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/secrets&quot;);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});</pre>\n<p class=\"caption\">secrets.ejs output tag (key line)</p><pre data-lang=\"html\">&lt;p class=&quot;secret-text&quot;&gt;&lt;%= secret %&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</pre>\n<h2>The task: Finish the Secrets app</h2>\n<div class=\"task-box\">\n<p><b>Starting point:</b> Working Express/Passport app with local and Google login. index.js has TODO comments for the /secrets query, the /submit GET route and the /submit POST route; submit.ejs exists; users table has no secret column.</p>\n<p><b>Goal:</b> Authenticated users can submit one secret that is stored in their users row and shown on /secrets.</p>\n<p><b>Steps</b></p><ol><li>Add column secret of type TEXT to the users table.</li><li>Change secrets.ejs to print &lt;%= secret %&gt; instead of fixed text.</li><li>Create GET /submit that renders submit.ejs only if req.isAuthenticated(), else redirects to /login.</li><li>Create POST /submit that reads req.body.secret and updates the row WHERE email = req.user.email, then redirects to /secrets.</li><li>Update GET /secrets to select the user's secret and pass it to the template, with a default when null.</li><li>Test login, submit, logout, login again, with both local and Google accounts.</li></ol>\n<p><b>Expected result:</b> Each user sees their latest saved secret after login; users without one see the default text; the submit page is inaccessible when logged out.</p>\n</div>\n<p class=\"caption\">Solution</p><pre data-lang=\"js\">// GET /secrets: show the logged-in user's own secret\napp.get(&quot;/secrets&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (req.isAuthenticated()) {\n    try {\n      const result = await db.query(\n        &quot;SELECT secret FROM users WHERE email = $1&quot;,\n        [req.user.email]\n      );\n      const secret = result.rows[0].secret;\n      if (secret) {\n        res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;, { secret: secret });\n      } else {\n        res.render(&quot;secrets.ejs&quot;, { secret: &quot;Jack Bauer is my hero.&quot; });\n      }\n    } catch (err) {\n      console.log(err);\n    }\n  } else {\n    res.redirect(&quot;/login&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\n// GET /submit: only for authenticated users\napp.get(&quot;/submit&quot;, (req, res) =&gt; {\n  if (req.isAuthenticated()) {\n    res.render(&quot;submit.ejs&quot;);\n  } else {\n    res.redirect(&quot;/login&quot;);\n  }\n});\n\n// POST /submit: store the secret on the user's own row\napp.post(&quot;/submit&quot;, async (req, res) =&gt; {\n  const submittedSecret = req.body.secret;\n  try {\n    await db.query(&quot;UPDATE users SET secret = $1 WHERE email = $2&quot;, [\n      submittedSecret,\n      req.user.email,\n    ]);\n    res.redirect(&quot;/secrets&quot;);\n  } catch (err) {\n    console.log(err);\n  }\n});</pre>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Forgetting to make route handlers async when using await.</li><li>Passing the email as a bare value instead of an array to db.query parameters.</li><li>Reading req.user in POST /submit without any auth check: if logged out, req.user is undefined and the handler throws. (Extra) Add the isAuthenticated guard there as well.</li><li><span class=\"extra\">Extra:</span> In this folder the starter index.js is identical to solution.js (already solved), so the TODO markers described in the lecture are not present in the repo copy.</li><li>Input name must be 'secret' to match req.body.secret.</li><li>Not redirecting after the update leaves the browser on a POST response.</li></ul>");
PG.notes("l276", "<p class=\"summary\">Short career tip on starting freelance web development: accumulate a portfolio and client references on low-paying marketplaces first, then move to higher-paying platforms.</p>\n<h3>Starting out as a freelancer</h3>\n<ul><li>Early obstacle: landing good contracts is hard without references or sample work, so the first goal is a portfolio plus clients who can vouch for you.</li><li>Begin on low-budget marketplaces such as Fiverr to practise dealing with real clients: working out what they need, communicating clearly, and delivering what they asked for.</li><li>Low pay at this stage is acceptable because the value is real projects, reviews and experience.</li><li>As skills and portfolio grow, move to platforms such as Upwork, Guru or Freelancer and charge more.</li><li>Rough timeline: about a year of steady small jobs should yield enough finished projects and testimonials to aim for premium clients.</li></ul>\n<h2>Common mistakes</h2>\n<ul><li>Pitching for top-paying gigs with no portfolio or references.</li></ul>");
